        <body>
            <div xml:id="d103">

                <pb facs="0304" n="267" sameAs="frus:frus1917-72PubDipv06/pg267"/>

                <head>103. Letter From the Director of the United States Information Agency (Murrow)
                    to the Assistant Secretary of State for Far Eastern Affairs (Harriman)<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d103-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1963-01-03">January 3, 1963</date>.</dateline>
                    <salute>Dear Averell,</salute>
                </opener>
                <p>You may be familiar with all of the events set forth in the enclosed memorandum.
                    I find the developments disturbing, and it appears that the situation may
                    continue to deteriorate.</p>
                <p>Is there anything we can do about it?</p>
                <p>Sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#bold">Edward R. Murrow</hi><anchor n="2"
                            xml:id="d103-f2"/></signed>
                </closer>



                <xi:include href="d103-a1.xml" xpointer="d103-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d103-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d103-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 306, DIRCTR Sub Files, 1963–69, Bx 6–29 63–69: Acc:
                    #72A5121, Entry UD WW 257, Box 8, FIELD—Far East (IAF). Confidential. Drafted by
                    Murrow. A copy was sent to Wilson.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d103-f2" n="2" xml:id="d103-f2-content">
                <p>Printed from a copy that bears this typed signature.</p>
            </note>
